==================================================
Lab 3: SQL Injection UNION Attack - Determining Number
of Columns Returned by the Query
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

A shopping website filters products by category, the same way as lab one. This time the goal is not just to
remove a condition from the query, it is to use the injection to pull in extra results from a different query
entirely, using something called a UNION attack. But before we can do that, we first need to find out exactly
how many columns the original query is returning, because that is a requirement for a UNION attack to work at
all.

Goal: find out how many columns the original SQL query selects.

==============================
WHAT IS A UNION ATTACK

A UNION attack lets an attacker combine the results of two separate SELECT queries into one single result. The
original query the website runs fetches product details. With a UNION attack, we can make the database also run
our own second query, and combine its output with the original results, so the extra data shows up on the same
page.

=======================================
WHY WE NEED TO KNOW THE COLUMN COUNT

For a UNION to work in SQL, both queries being combined must return the exact same number of columns. If the
original query returns say three columns, and our injected query only returns two or four, the database will
throw an error and refuse to run it. So before running any real UNION attack, we must first discover exactly how
many columns the original query uses.

=============================
THE TECHNIQUE: USING NULL

To find the column count, we repeatedly inject a UNION SELECT statement filled with the word NULL, written as
many times as the number of columns we are testing for. NULL works for this because NULL can fit into any column
type, whether it expects text, numbers, or dates. So instead of guessing actual data types, we just use NULL as a
placeholder for every column.

We start by testing with one NULL, then two NULLs, then three, and so on. Each time, if the number of NULLs does
not match the real column count, the database will return an error and the page will not load normally, or the
lab will not register as solved. Once the number of NULLs we send matches the actual number of columns in the
original query, the database accepts the UNION, no error happens, and the lab recognizes this as solved.

======================================
WHAT THE PAYLOAD LOOKS LIKE

For testing with three columns as an example, the category value sent would be: a valid category name, followed
by a single quote, then the word UNION, then SELECT, then NULL comma NULL comma NULL, then two dashes.

The two dashes at the end comment out anything that follows in the original query, the same trick used in the
earlier labs.

======================================
WHY WE NEED A VALID CATEGORY NAME

The category value we send still needs to start with a real, existing category name, such as Corporate gifts.
This is because some database setups, including the one in this lab, only run the UNION part of the query if the
first part of the WHERE condition can still succeed or at least be valid, depending on how the query is
structured. Using a real category name keeps the first part of the query working normally, while our injected
UNION part runs alongside it.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and go to a product category page.
In the category value, submit a real category name, followed by a single quote, then UNION SELECT NULL--
If that gives an error, increase the number of NULLs by one each time.
Keep increasing the NULLs and resubmitting until no error appears, meaning you found the exact column count.
Once the correct number of NULLs is found, the lab is marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

It imports the requests library, used for sending web requests.

A session is created so that cookies stay consistent across each test, the same way a browser would behave
while reloading a page multiple times.

It asks you to paste the category filter address, and saves it as filter_url.

It sets valid_category to the text Corporate gifts, which is a real category name that exists in this lab, so
the first part of the query keeps working while we test the UNION part.

It then loops from one up to ten, trying column counts one at a time.

On each loop, it builds a string of NULL values separated by commas, repeated as many times as the current
number being tested. For example, on the third loop, this becomes NULL comma NULL comma NULL.

It builds the full payload using the valid category name, a single quote, the word UNION SELECT, the NULL
values just built, and two dashes at the end to comment out the rest of the original query.

This payload is placed into the category parameter and sent to the website using the same session.

It checks if the response contains the sentence the lab displays when solved. If it does, it prints a success
message showing exactly how many columns were found, and stops the loop immediately using break.

If none of the ten attempts succeed, the else part connected to the for loop runs, which prints a failure message
suggesting the category name might be wrong or the lab link expired.

=============================
WHY THE FOR-ELSE STRUCTURE IS USED

In Python, a for loop can have an else part attached to it. This else only runs if the loop finishes completely
without ever hitting a break statement. Here, it is used so that the failure message only prints if none of the
ten attempts worked, since a successful attempt always triggers break and skips the else part entirely.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The website still builds the query by joining the category input directly into the SQL text, the same
underlying flaw as before. This time, instead of only changing the WHERE condition, the injected UNION SELECT
is accepted as a second query that can be joined with the first, as long as the number of columns matches
exactly.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries so user input can never be interpreted as part of the SQL structure, including UNION
statements.

Other good practices:

Disable detailed database error messages on production websites, since those errors are often what reveals
column count and structure to an attacker during this kind of testing.
Apply strict input validation so unexpected characters like quotes are rejected before ever reaching the query.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

A UNION attack requires both queries to return the exact same number of columns, or the database will reject it.
NULL is used as a safe placeholder value because it can fit into any column type.
Testing is usually done by trial and error, increasing the column count by one each time until no error appears.
This step alone does not extract any real data yet, it only tells us how many columns the real query uses, which
is needed before further UNION attacks can retrieve actual data
